A||||25||btoth||ABM Treaty Debate||||2||
Z||000000||btoth||09-24-2001||03:04 PM||||What do you think?  Should the US abrogate the ABM Treaty and build a national missile defense?<P>(This is a topic that I'm writing a debate paper about for my Political Science class. I can't use anything you write here in the paper, but it'd be interesting to see what you all think about this.)<P>Personally, I think it's a dumb move to abolish the treaty... but I'm still working on gathering enough facts to back that statement up.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||2
Z||000001||LarryB||09-24-2001||07:05 PM||||Unilaterally abrogating a treaty isn't something to be taken lightly, since in effect you're going back on your given word.<P>I don't think the current administration has made a sufficiently strong case to the public to warrant support for doing this.  That's not to say it isn't deserved, but IMO they haven't made their case yet.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Mach10||09-24-2001||08:10 PM||||Building such systems would tip the current balance of nuclear/ballistic arms. It WILL start another arms race, which as we all know, was bad in the past, and would be bad now. In addition, the US is talking about breaking a treaty. That can't be good for a countries reputation.<P>On the flip side, it's not like the US is making weapons of mass-destruction. On the contrary, they are creating a defence system. A country is allowed to have nuclear-capable ICBMs, but isn't allowed to create countermeasures? Sounds a bit silly. "No butcher-backs!"<P>It all boils down to what other countries think, really. If everyone wants to make a concerned effort to making ICBMs worthless, that may spark a global disarmament. But then again, maybe not.||||reg||7
Z||000003||Red2m4||09-25-2001||10:48 PM||||<BR>I think it's a bad idea.  I think a missle defense system would be over priced an innefective.  If some one wants to nuke the country, it'll be by boat, or even car.  Missles are very expensive, to luanch, to build.<P>There was a really good article about this in "Journal of Foreign Affairs".  Dont remember exactly when, maybe 2nd half of last year.  Could maybe use it for your class.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Butter||09-26-2001||03:55 PM||||The U.S. is at war.<BR>It should do whatever it needs to do to uphold its constitution.<BR>If it appears to need such a defensive machine then it should procure it.||||reg||4
Z||000005||Wolfhound||09-26-2001||04:32 PM||||So far they have faked every successful test they have had (Two). I doubt the enemy will put GPS homing devices on their missiles.<BR>the money can be better spent.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Tonker||09-26-2001||05:25 PM||||I think it's generally a bad idea. A lot of people have mentioned good points so far. Abrogation of a treaty is not to be taken lightly. It would be prohibitively expensive without any concrete proof that it would provide total coverage. In addition there are many oher ways to attack with weapons of mass destruction as was mentioned.<P>What I would add is that during the Cold War, deterrence was achieved largely by Mutually Assured Destruction. One state might lauch its weapons first, but both countries (if not the a significant portion of the world) would be destroyed in the retaliatory strike.<P>If the US develops ABM capability then it will alter the balance of power. This in itself is not necessarily a bad thing once the Missile Shield is fully developed, however in the intervening years the other states, most specifically China and Russia are presented with a dilemma. Once the US develops such a shield, it will be much less at risk of nuclear attack from other nations, and as a result there is less to stop America from attacking or dictating behaviour to another state. The dilemma then for the other states is a "use them or lose them" situation, which in the short term at least increases the possibility of nuclear war.<P>The other side of the coin is that these states could develop their own ABM defence if they have they financial and technical resources required to do so. The results of this would likely be an arms race in space, to develop the greatest capacity to destroy the other state's ability to protect itself from your missiles.<P>Basically, I can see where abrogation of the ABM treaty could get very messy and costly, with little real return on investment. Realistically as things stand, while nuclear attack by another nation is an unpleasant background thought, it is unlikely given the current state of affairs. A more likely nuclear threat is from terrorist groups that are acting within the US's borders. ABM defence would do nothing to prevent this.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Tonker (edited 09-26-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||maryjane||09-26-2001||06:49 PM||||I've tried to stay out of this one because I'm not really familar with the terms of the SALT treaties, from which the current agreements stem. Throughout history, from stones to spears to arrows to guns and on and on, it is a given that if a weapon can be designed and implemented, it will be by someone. The U.S., with help from scientists all over the world, built, tested, and used the first nuclear weapon. BUT, if we hadn't, there is a high possibility that someone else would have.The same probably holds true for the proposed system. If the Chinese, Israeli's, Russians, Afgan's or Iraqi's had the money and technology to build it, I'm pretty sure they would, treaty or not. I had heard at one time that if we developed a defensive umbrella, the technology would be shared with all nations. Don't know if this still holds true. Like I say, I haven't kept up with this. I would rather have something in place that would work for defence, even if it meant the opposing nations had the same.As it stands right now, attack from China or Russia seems unlikely. I can't say the same for Iraq, Libya, North Korea, or Islamic extremists. (Note:I said extremists.<BR>Not all Islamic believers). And I think that extremists would settle for an attack on one of our allies , if they couldn't get to us. A rouge nation attack would likely be targeted at the N.E&gt; (D.C. or NY). Would affect parts of Canada far more than it would me here in West Texas. If it bothers you that much, Tonker, Mach10, to violate a treaty or develop a new system, perhaps Canada can work out their own treaty with the proviso that it not be used in their defence if it comes to it. At least they can die with  principles and ethics intact. It will sound nice at the eulogy. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> <p>[This message has been edited by maryjane (edited 09-26-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||Mach10||09-26-2001||08:06 PM||||MJ: Nice to see you back in the military arguments  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>I argued for and against. I think that defensive weapons (does that sound weird to you too?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> ) do have a great place in warfare. My only concern right now is tipping the balance of power one way or another. You are right, though. Someone WILL build it eventually. Probably, the US should be the ones to do it. However, it should be done carefully so as not to piss everyone off... But then, the treaty part is not a strong point. If everyone agrees to dump it, then it's hardly a bad thing... <BR>I'll remain undecided. On one hand, it's security, on the other, it could start another arms race. I don't like being on "high-alert" all the time. Count me as sitting on a fence, whistling Oh-Canada.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Ooh! I hope *I'M* Mentioned in the national Eulogy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||10
Z||000009||maryjane||09-26-2001||10:21 PM||||Sean, I'm not arguing--yet. Defensive weapons; yeah sounds kinda strange but brings up an intresting thought. What if a war was fought like that? You shoot one at me- I shoot it down., I shoot 2 at you you shoot one down and one goes in the ocean. This goes on till we're out of weapons, the one with the most 'hits', wins. Nobody dies.<BR>Seems I saw something like this on an early Star Trek once. <BR>Computerized warfare where casualties were calculated by probable strikes. Only thing was, you had to send x amount of people to extermination chambers everytime the computers said you were hit. Oh well. Nevah happen here.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Tonker||09-26-2001||10:54 PM||||Glad to see you back in the fray, Don!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>It's not that I have anything against an ABM shield. It's just that, first of all, violation of a treaty sets a bad example. It might come back to haunt us at some point. Second, it might not really accomplish anything. It may work against rogue states who don't have the resources to compete. If that is the sole purpose of going forward with it then fine. It will probably accomplish the objective. At the same time you have to consider that in the short term it might increase the possibility of nuclear war (see my previous post). <P>I would suggest, though, that if the US is going to proceed with this plan, it is best to do it now and quickly. Since we're no longer in the Cold War and China seems only mildly concerned about it at this point, it's probably as safe a time to do it as any. <P>I stand by what I said earlier, I think it's a bad idea in general, but I might have misinterpreted the focus of the plan. It's risky to do something like this, but then as was mentioned, at some point somebody else would probably do it anyways. Better to be the first than the last. At the same time, in principle I support an ABM defence system. If it can be done safely with only mild opposition from the international community, then great. I think, probably it can, and as long as you only expect it to protect you from smaller rogue states then it's probably feasible too.<P>If I were to guess what would happen, I would say the most resistance would come from Russia and China. At the same time, they probably wouldn't try to prevent it, but instead develop their own systems. If this happens it means that your ABM defence would have the desireable effect of reducing the risk of launch by small rogue nations, but would probably spark a space arms race among the major powers. A missile shield would probably have little effect against the major powers in this case. <P>Don, I saw the episode of Star Trek that you referred to, and it was an excellent one. Hokey in the Original Series' own way, but it had an interesting message. Good call.<P>BTW did anyone watch Enterprise tonight? Loved it!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR>||||reg||14
Z||000011||maryjane||09-26-2001||11:47 PM||||These are the sticking points on ABM that prevent implementation, testing, or transfer of missle defense.<BR>ART I: prohibits the deployment of a system capable of defending ALL of the U.S. territory against missle attack, and even building the base for such territorial defense.<P>ART III: as amended by a 1974 protocol, prohibits the deployment of more than 100 fixed, land-based interceptors and even at that limit, allows those only at a single specific site in the U.S., which the U.S. desiginated as Grand Forks, North Dakota. (near a large number of ICBM sites) The Russians chose to designate their 100 interceptor site as Moscow.( the site at Grand Forks has since been deactivated as it was deemed too expensive and ineffective)<P>ART V: prohibits not only the deployment, but even the development and subsequent testing of sea-based, space-based, air-based, or mobile land-based systems.<P>ART VI: prohibits giving non-ABM systems an ABM capability or even testing them in an ABM mode<P>ART IX: prohibits transfer of ABM systems or components to other countries.<BR> These are articles that are causing the problems for the U.S. in their quest for a nationwide defense. However, Russia has already violated another Article of ABM which says that all long range ABM connected<BR>radar must be located on that country's border and 'point' outward so as not to be used as an ABM radar. They built a large ABM radar site at Krasnoyarsk, roughly 800 KM's<BR>from their border in '84 and refused to dismantle for nearly a decade. Also, the ABM treaty is between only the USSR and the United States. Many say the dissolution of the USSR into it's component countries makes the treaty 'null & void'. Open to interpetation.<BR>The treaty, as ammended and protocoled, prevents use, implementation, testing or designing any system that goes faster than 5MPS or farther than 3500 miles, or targets any missile in the 'boost' phase. This leaves room for 'theatre missle defence' (TMD's) such as the Patriot. Both sides make use of this feature. It serves to keep a conventional battle from escalating into a nuclear war. (Few believe that a theatre nuclear strike would be self containing) If you could shoot down an in theatre nuke, there would be no nuclear explosion, therefore, no retalitory strike. However, the speed limit and range limit would prevent the implementation of any defense against 'rouge nations' unless they were launched closer than 3500 miles or slower than 5MPS. The reason that no rouge nation or China is complaining much about SDI, is that they aren't part or parcel of the ABM treaty to start with. Only the U.S. and the USSR. The section providing for mutual inspection, and sharing of technology remains intact. Russia has already proposed a joint worldwide ABM system, to protect every country from rouge nation attack, as long as their technology was used in conjunction with ours. We just don't trust them yet.<p>[This message has been edited by maryjane (edited 09-27-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||Mach10||09-27-2001||01:10 AM||||You know, I wrote a story that sort of pertains to this, back in High School. I'll summerize:<P>A small company sets up shop, and has a reasonable number of very talented individuals. They build computer stuff, guidance systems, and develop defense contracts nationally, then globally. <BR>When they make enough cash, they buy a group of uninhabited islands in the south pacific from their respective owners, and set up a power plant and some manufacturing plants. At first it starts as a tax haven (the lease being far cheaper than the taxes) to maximize profits. Then, when things start getting interestingly profitable, they declare independance and pay off the owning government a SICK sum of money, as well as supplying them weapons. After this, they start taking applications for employment globally. Only the best and brightest (and their families) are in. [at this point, the reason for the story; a political essay on the Uutopian society]. Production goes off the charts, obscene new defense systems developed. <BR>The overall point is that this area, a few hundred sq. kilometers becomes the dominating world power. Nothing can get close enough to the islands to attack them, they have almost instant striking capability to anywhere in the world. What happens is that this little island can (and does) intervene with any inter-continental attack. World peace is instituited by a population of less than 100,000 people, and some pretty wacky sci-fi weapons that they are totally free to develop. The people in control are peaceful, and enlightened. No strife exists (based on some radical social changes I made... It's LONG ) within the island, and the rest of the world cannot start any non-conventional wars.<P>I got a 97% Transactional English mark for this peice of work.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Just thought I'd share it  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>MJ: Speed Limit?!? "I'm sorry sir, I'm going to have to ticket you. I clocked your missile at 5.2kps, .2kps over the posted limit. You damn near broke my radar!"<P>I'm not familiar at all with the treaties, so I can't comment. I'll just say that I don't like the balance of power shifts (as bad ****  usually happens), but I don't have any moral objection to building DEFENSES. <P>Why doesn't the US get off it's prat and design a Force Sheild System like on Star-Trek?!? now THAT'D be useful...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||7
Z||000013||maryjane||09-27-2001||01:27 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Mach10:<BR><B>You know, I wrote a story that sort of pertains to this, back in High School. I'll summerize:<P>Originally posted by Mach10:<BR>I'm not familiar at all with the treaties, . <P>Why doesn't the US get off it's prat and design a Force Sheild System like on Star-Trek?!? now THAT'D be useful...   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>I'm not familar with them either, had to do a quick read on it. Pretty dry. <BR>Why doesn't Canada get off it's 'prat?' and build one itself? (wth is a prat? don't answer that, I don't think I want to know!)||||reg||1
Z||000014||Mach10||09-27-2001||12:07 PM||||"Prat" is English-English slang for "a$$"<P>Why don't we do it first?!? Fine! See if I don't! *Runs off in direction of High Voltage Labs*||||reg||10
Z||000015||fogglethorpe||09-27-2001||09:16 PM||||The ABM treaty is void for two reasons:<BR>1) The Soviet Union, with whom we signed the treaty, no longer exists.<BR>2) When the Soviet Union was still an entity, they violated the hell out of the treaty.<P>I say, America first.<P>It is disturbing to me that we have to debate this at all. ||||reg||1
Z||000016||JohnnyK||09-28-2001||02:00 AM||||There are two sides in my books. First, if it works, then "in a way" it is a good idea. BUT<P>It won't work (Didn't they explain once that another country could fire off about 1000 missles and only one of them having a nuclear warhead, and the system would be ineffective)<P>You are breaking a treaty, and it is a bad idea when you want other countries on your side.||||reg||1
Z||000017||LarryB||09-28-2001||04:06 AM||||They've got a LONG way to go before coming up with something that really works.  As was previously pointed out, the tests based on recent research were based on "controlled" (read: rigged) conditions, nothing that even came close to a real-world senario.  Putting an effective and coordinated system in place will cost a staggering fortune and we would have to sacrifice elsewhere both in the military and the other budgets to fund it.  Are we anywhere near being at the point where it's actually worth doing it? IMO, no.  <P>Look at the the full history of the Patriot missile as an example; the info coming out the last few years indicates that in reality they weren't anywhere as effective during the Gulf War as we were originally led to believe. I haven't seen anything showing we've able to do significantly better with the latest and greatest.<P>I'm also inherently skeptical of anyone who tries to sh-itcan proven-to-be-effective weapons and replace them with something cool and expensive and sexy...but that doesn't work as well(2 examples: the decision in the 1950s to eliminate guns and go with strictly missiles on fighters (the Phantom), which the planners found early on in Vietnam to be a Really Bad Idea, and before the Gulf War (and still!) the attempts to eliminate the A-10 in favor of the F-16 as the military's ground-attack aircraft.  Despite all the glowing PR spewed to the contrary the F-16 proved to be terribly inept at that role, it flies too fast and the pilots won't fly the craft low enough to the ground to be a reliably effective ground attack craft.  The A-10 on the other hand proved what Schwarzkof already knew. It was well-suited for the role and was a remarkably effective tank-killer, it flew low and slow and its gatling gun with bullets made of depleted uranium (heavier than lead) ripped anything in its path including tanks to shreds.<P>Lastly, does anyone else have any old Nike missile sites in their town? That program didn't last too long either, it was already in its death throes by the time SALT I was signed. ||||reg||1
Z||000018||Sleeper||09-28-2001||04:45 AM||||I hold the firm belief that whatever the military shows you it has is decades behind what it actually has..in other words..black projects.abm treaty? it's probaly already negated||||reg||1
Z||000019||maryjane||09-28-2001||10:37 AM||||LarryB, There are several old underground missle silos around the area here, mostly  around Abilene. I was a kid when they were building them, but I remember seeing a sign, I think it said NIKE-ZEUS.Some were different then others. We used to go down in them, after the AF left them. Rooms are on springs, you get enough people in them, & run from side to side you can make the rooms shake. When they were abandoned, the local school boards were given control of them. They sold them to individuals. Some were used for grain storage, but there is at least one that has been made into a pretty nice home. The control rooms and military living qtrs are the living room and such, and the missle bay has been turned into a heck of a workshop. Whole thing stays a constant temp all yr round. Saw a thing on TV where one had filled with water, and a local guy was renting it out to dive clubs to explore. ||||reg||1
Z||000020||Mach10||09-28-2001||11:23 AM||||MJ: I couldn't help but laugh at the thought of a younger MJ wacking a hula-hoop with a stick inside one...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Sorry  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>However, I find it both neat and somewhat disturbed that part of a weapon of mass-destruction ends up a playground. Sword to a plowshare, huh?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>It'd be nice if everyone turned their weapons into toys. Think of it: one HUGE paintball match to settle the Jerusalem problem "I got you! Ay-gavolt!"  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||10
