Z||000024||LarryB||09-28-2001||02:12 PM||||There were several of them north of Boston. Nike sites were in 2 parts, the actual missile silos and the control areas.  Because of the way the tracking system worked the silo had to be at least 3000' away from the control area, where the crew and radar equipment were located.<P>The ones I all know of have either been abandoned or they were filled in and the land used to build condos on.  The abandoned ones are full of water; Massachusetts in general has a very high water table. <P>And yeah, Zeus was a later class of Nike missile, from memory it carried a 20 kiloton nuclear warhead and it was intended to shoot down incoming ballistic missles. <P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by maryjane:<BR><B>LarryB, There are several old underground missle silos around the area here, mostly  around Abilene. I was a kid when they were building them, but I remember seeing a sign, I think it said NIKE-ZEUS.Some were different then others. We used to go down in them, after the AF left them. Rooms are on springs, you get enough people in them, & run from side to side you can make the rooms shake. When they were abandoned, the local school boards were given control of them. They sold them to individuals. Some were used for grain storage, but there is at least one that has been made into a pretty nice home. The control rooms and military living qtrs are the living room and such, and the missle bay has been turned into a heck of a workshop. Whole thing stays a constant temp all yr round. Saw a thing on TV where one had filled with water, and a local guy was renting it out to dive clubs to explore. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>||||reg||1
